WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.200
Mailchimp.
Basically every business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.040
small and large, used this tool,&nbsp;
but now it feels like no one does.&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.760
13 million users. The biggest bootstrapped&nbsp;
startup exit in history, sold for $12 billion.&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.080
But, now it’s dying.
Growth is flat, revenue is down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:26.320
and users are fleeing to competitors.
And, it’s entirely Mailchimp’s fault.&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.160
It’s gotten so bad, Intuit now&nbsp;
reports their earnings twice.&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.800
Once with Mailchimp. And&nbsp;
once without Mailchimp… Yeah.&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.520
Mailchimp had it all. It was the&nbsp;
king, so how did it collapse?&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:47.200
It turns out, the thing that propelled&nbsp;
Mailchimp to the top, is what killed it…

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.160
If you ran a small business in&nbsp;
2005 and wanted to send an email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.520
to your customers, you had a few options.
BCC hundreds of email addresses in Outlook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.840
which never looks very professional,&nbsp;
and will definitely end up in spam.&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.960
You could spend hundreds of&nbsp;
dollars hiring a skilled developer.&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.440
Or, you were left with buying expensive,&nbsp;
complicated software built for companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.520
with an entire marketing department.
Not great for a small company.&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:21.280
This was a constant complaint from Ben Chestnut&nbsp;
and Dan Kurzius' clients, who kept asking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.640
help with email marketing instead of focusing on&nbsp;
the web design services their agency provided.&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.200
Ben and Dan saw the opportunity.&nbsp;
There’s a clear gap in the market.&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.840
So, they repurposed code from a failed e-greeting&nbsp;
card product and turned it into a fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:41.280
simple tool for sending emails: Mailchimp.
Little did they know, this side project would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.200
soon become a software company.
But, what’s also surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.040
is how these two were way ahead of their time.
For years, Mailchimp was a mostly paid product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.440
with minor free features.
But in 2009, they went with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.200
the Freemium model, which they called “Forever&nbsp;
Free”. 500 contacts, which rapidly increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.640
to 2,000 within three years.
Great deal. So why so generous?&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.640
First, the free plan was basically&nbsp;
free advertising for Mailchimp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.560
Every email sent on the free plan had a&nbsp;
Mailchimp badge in the footer. Customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.680
could click it and sign up for Mailchimp.
Second, even though they benefited from the free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.880
plan, they offered free templates, free tutorials,&nbsp;
and free automation to help customers grow.&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.240
Once your audience outgrew the&nbsp;
free plan, Mailchimp charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.400
you more as your audience grew bigger.
So, Mailchimp only made more money when your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:39.200
business was actually growing. Pretty genius.
And boy it worked. Users went from 85,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.760
to 450,000 in just 12 months.
This free plan attracted more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:51.360
paying customers, increasing them by over&nbsp;
150% and generating a 650% increase in profit.&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.640
“I use MailChimp for free right now, but I&nbsp;
can't wait until my list is big enough for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.560
me to start having to pay them. It'll mean&nbsp;
that I have enough subscribers to actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.360
monetize the list. At that point, I'll be&nbsp;
more than happy to give them my credit card.”&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.840
Honestly, it was pretty great… for now.
So, what happens when competitors stop selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:16.560
you a better email tool... and start selling you&nbsp;
a better way to make money from those emails?&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.840
To target developers, in 2012 they launched&nbsp;
Mandrill, a cheap, standalone transaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.720
email service that helps send automatic emails&nbsp;
like verification emails, order confirmations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.240
and password reset emails.
But a surprisingly big break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.880
came from an unexpected place.
In 2014, Mailchimp sponsored a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.680
podcast called Serial.
During the ad read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.120
Serial involved random people on the street.
But one, didn’t quite get the name right.&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
This, quickly, became a meme.
Then even an SNL sketch.&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.200
It was a pretty big marketing win for Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.440
They even bought MailKimp.com and&nbsp;
redirected it to their homepage.&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.840
By 2016, Mailchimp was making&nbsp;
over $400 million in revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:21.680
with around 550 employees, 16 million users.
246 billion emails sent that year.&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:29.040
And they had done it without venture capital.&nbsp;
No external funding. Pretty great bootstrapping!&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.880
For a while Mailchimp was&nbsp;
the underdog everyone liked.&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.800
I remember a time where every startup but also&nbsp;
older, small businesses, were using Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.440
They were at the top, how&nbsp;
did things fall off so bad?&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:05:20.400
Well, Mailchimp had a problem.
Sounds good, but… companies have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.520
a “moat” for a reason.
But there’s more.&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:35.040
Mailchimp would gradually make things&nbsp;
worse for everyone, starting with Mandrill.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.520
Tools like Mailchimp, and the better email&nbsp;
tools today, show it’s one of the easiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.640
times to start a business. You have a tool to&nbsp;
help you with just about anything you need.&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.920
One of the best decisions I ever made was starting&nbsp;
my own business, and I don’t think I would’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.640
achieved any financial freedom without it.
Building a passive income stream or starting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.622
side business is especially easy, with Printify.
Printify allows you to build your very own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.622 --> 00:06:04.560
ecommerce brand, in minutes,&nbsp;
and with no upfront investment.&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.160
Use it to build a passive income&nbsp;
stream or start a business.&nbsp;

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:13.120
But with Printify, you can really build&nbsp;
something of your own. Your own style.&nbsp;

