Thank you to Printify for sponsoring this video.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.080
Mandrill users woke up to a strange&nbsp;
update on 24th February 2016.&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.640
Developers had just 60 days to move their&nbsp;
email lists and rework their systems to connect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.120
Mandrill to a paid Mailchimp account.
They now needed a paid Mailchimp plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.000
starting at $10 a month, plus Mandrill's&nbsp;
email credits doubled in price.&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:39.680
(Developer) "This is not to say that I’ve been&nbsp;
unhappy with Mandrill, the services offered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.560
the economics of it, documentation, etc. It’s&nbsp;
just that our usage is really closer to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.600
notification scenario and the traffic volume&nbsp;
for our biking organization doesn’t justify a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:54.000
$20 a month cost. Heck, that’s what&nbsp;
its costing me to run the whole thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.960
end to end and I think that's high."
Many developers left for SendGrid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.720
Postmark, Mailgun, or Amazon SES.
For Mailchimp, this was unfortunately the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:11.280
playbook... making money by taking something away.
But this was just the beginning. Mailchimp made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.440
three major, and strange moves in 2019.
First, was a weird feud with… Shopify?&nbsp;

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:22.160
They asked Shopify to pull the Mailchimp&nbsp;
app from the Shopify App Store. So why?&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.360
Shopify was becoming the home&nbsp;
for millions of online stores,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:30.160
and Mailchimp had an integration that let those&nbsp;
stores send their customer data into Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.480
A customer buys something from your Shopify&nbsp;
store... their information goes into Mailchimp...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.800
and you can send them targeted emails.
Easy, right?&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:44.640
But then...after 18 months of back and forth,&nbsp;
Shopify removed Mailchimp from its App Store.&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:49.280
According to Mailchimp, Shopify changed its terms&nbsp;
and was asking for access to far more customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.360
data than Mailchimp was willing to hand over.
(Mailchimp): “Anything that hurts our customers’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.640
privacy was a non-starter for us. We had no way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.600
getting that consent from&nbsp;
our users retroactively,”&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.320
But on Shopify’s end:
(Shopify): “Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:06.640
Mailchimp refuses to synchronize customer&nbsp;
information captured on merchants’ online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:11.360
stores and email opt-out preferences.&nbsp;
As a result, our merchants, other apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:17.200
and partner ecosystems can’t reliably serve their&nbsp;
customers or comply with privacy legislation.”&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.680
Both sides blamed each other, but Shopify&nbsp;
merchants using Mailchimp were caught in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.800
the middle, forced to either find another&nbsp;
email provider or move their store elsewhere.&nbsp;

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.960
So why does this matter?
Well, Mailchimp had just created an opening for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.720
one of its biggest future competitors: Klaviyo.&nbsp;
And this would hurt pretty bad down the line.&nbsp;

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.000
For now, Mailchimp was going&nbsp;
through a metamorphosis.&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.640
It revamped its entire brand from an email&nbsp;
company into a full marketing platform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:50.800
They now offered websites, landing&nbsp;
pages, social ads, CRM… and postcards?&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.240
It estimated its revenue would reach $700 million&nbsp;
by the year's end, with new tiers at $0, $9.99,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.280
$14.99, and $299.
Mailchimp had spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:05.840
years becoming really good at one thing.
Now it was trying to become good at everything.&nbsp;

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:09.200
And this was the beginning&nbsp;
of the end for Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:13.360
Then came perhaps the strangest&nbsp;
and most blatant act of greed:&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.640
In October of that same year,&nbsp;
Mailchimp changed its pricing from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:23.600
subscribed contacts to your entire audience.
And this was really, really bad for customers.&nbsp;

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:28.240
That meant unsubscribed people, and even&nbsp;
contacts who never opted into marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.840
could count toward your bill.
You're telling me I can be charged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:37.120
for someone I'm not even allowed to email?
Well, to Mailchimp, they were no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.760
just an email company. Someone might&nbsp;
unsubscribe from email... but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.080
could still be reached through a postcard,&nbsp;
a social ad, or another marketing channel.&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:50.800
After all, Mailchimp is much more&nbsp;
than an email company now, right guys?&nbsp;

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:57.120
For some businesses, the bills skyrocketed.&nbsp;
One analysis estimated a 258% increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.440
for a 10,000-contact customer.
So, what came of all of this?&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:05.120
Was it worth it?
Did Mailchimp become massively profitable?&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:09.920
Well, despite all those changes...&nbsp;
Mailchimp kept growing… for now.&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:15.680
From $525 million in revenue in 2017 to&nbsp;
$600 million in 2018, $700 million in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:20.800
and $800 million in 2020.
So maybe the strategy worked?&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.320
Well, remember, something else happened in 2020.
With everyone stuck inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.520
it makes sense revenue jumped.
But, it didn’t jump that much.&nbsp;

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:34.320
In the US alone, e-commerce&nbsp;
sales jumped from roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:43.680
$570 billion in 2019 to $815 billion in 2020.
Shopify's revenue grew 81.9% in just nine months.&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:48.880
Meanwhile, the largest email marketing&nbsp;
company on earth grew just 14% during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:55.760
that time. 14%! During the pandemic!
But, Mailchimp was extremely valuable.&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:00.560
In September 2021, Intuit bought it for&nbsp;
$12 billion, the largest acquisition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:06.880
a company that had never taken outside funding.
But, what exactly did Intuit buy for $12 billion?&nbsp;

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:13.840
Mailchimp had 13 million users, but only&nbsp;
800,000 paying customers. That's just 6%.&nbsp;

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:17.360
With all those prior changes…&nbsp;
wouldn’t that be larger?&nbsp;

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:21.680
Unfortunately for Intuit,&nbsp;
Mailchimp was long past its prime.&nbsp;

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:32.800
And it only got worse from here.
If you’ve watched this far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:37.440
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.&nbsp;

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.480
Mailchimp soon had a much&nbsp;
bigger problem on its hands.&nbsp;

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:47.040
Remember Shopify? Those merchants still&nbsp;
needed an email platform; just not Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:52.480
This brings us back to Klaviyo. It was built&nbsp;
for e-commerce. It prioritised order history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.880
lifetime value, and browsing behaviour.
A store owner could see exactly how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:04.240
revenue each email generated, and this is huge.
It makes sense they raised 150 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:09.280
but that’s not the important part.
In August 2022, Shopify invested $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:14.720
million in Klaviyo, made it the recommended&nbsp;
email partner for Shopify Plus, and gave it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.560
early access to new features.
Why does this matter? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:23.120
Klaviyo was laser-focused on the&nbsp;
thing that matters: Conversions.&nbsp;

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:27.040
It’s built for e-commerce, and&nbsp;
for making its customers money.&nbsp;

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:31.440
And while Klaviyo was winning, Mailchimp's&nbsp;
13 million users were about to discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:36.640
the new meaning of “Forever Free.”
Mailchimp's free plan had been remarkably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:43.040
generous. So, naturally the cuts started.
Paid plans jumped from $9.99 to $13 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:48.080
$14.99 to $20, while email scheduling&nbsp;
quietly disappeared from the free plan.&nbsp;

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:51.600
Then came March 10, 2023.
The free plan dropped from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:57.920
2,000 contacts to 500, while monthly&nbsp;
sends plunged from 10,000 to just 1,000.&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:04.400
That's a 75% cut in contacts and&nbsp;
a 90% cut in emails... yikes.&nbsp;

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:11.520
By mid-2025, automation was gone too. Then,&nbsp;
in February 2026, Mailchimp cut it again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:17.120
down to 250 contacts and 500 emails a month.
Go over the contact limit, and Mailchimp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:21.360
would put your account on hold,&nbsp;
stopping you from sending emails.&nbsp;

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:26.640
And in April, even legacy customers&nbsp;
saw their prices rise by up to 13%.&nbsp;

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.720
People don't mind paying for something new.
But take away something they've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:36.640
had for years... resentment builds up.
And while Mailchimp was trying to be everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:42.800
everyone else was becoming more specialised.
Klaviyo doubled down on e-commerce. In 2025,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:49.360
it reported $1.23 billion in revenue, up 32% from&nbsp;
the previous year, with nearly 3,900 customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:56.480
paying over $50,000 a year each. That's more&nbsp;
revenue than Mailchimp has ever publicly reported.&nbsp;

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:00.400
Creators moved to Beehiiv, Kit, and&nbsp;
Substack, where they could make money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:05.680
through paid subscriptions, referrals, and ads.
Meanwhile, budget-conscious businesses had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.800
MailerLite, Brevo, and Omnisend,&nbsp;
all offering generous free plans.&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:17.840
All of this left Mailchimp stuck at zero growth&nbsp;
since mid-2024, meanwhile, MailerLite, Omnisend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:24.640
HubSpot, Klaviyo, and Brevo were all growing.
Other platforms focused on one specific problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:29.680
or, if they were for “everything”, were way&nbsp;
more enterprise focused. Mailchimp hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:33.120
really found a niche that worked.
They were originally for small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:39.280
companies… but then… also not?
And then came the last nail in the coffin.&nbsp;

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:45.360
In August 2025, Intuit's CFO admitted that small&nbsp;
businesses, the “bread and butter” of Mailchimp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.320
found the product “a bit harder to use.”
Yeah you think?&nbsp;

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.240
Total users was down to 11&nbsp;
million. From a peak of 16.&nbsp;

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:59.120
Mailchimp's revenue was down, and&nbsp;
Intuit was cutting 17% of its workforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:03.920
more than 3,000 employees, and Mailchimp was&nbsp;
specifically named as one of the areas where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:09.200
the company would be reducing investment.
For a company Intuit bought for $12 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:14.800
that was a pretty significant change in direction.
Intuit also reported that its Global Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:19.040
Solutions revenue grew 15%.
Pretty good, right?&nbsp;

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:25.040
But excluding Mailchimp, it grew 17%.
In other words, Intuit had started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:29.571
showing investors the business with&nbsp;
Mailchimp... and without Mailchimp.&nbsp;

00:16:29.571 --> 00:16:31.920
Pretty telling sign, I think.
The company that Intuit bought for $12 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:37.040
was no longer being presented as a growth engine.
The product became more complicated and expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:41.680
for its users. The free plan became&nbsp;
less attractive. Meanwhile, specialised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:46.160
competitors got better and faster.
Mailchimp never had a moat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:50.160
which wasn’t inherently a problem.
But a moat sure helps when you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:55.760
okay at everything, but a master of none.
And when you continue to cut your plans.&nbsp;

00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:01.120
There are a lot of these tools which exploded&nbsp;
in the 2010s, yet are struggling today.&nbsp;

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:05.520
One of them is Slack. Yet, Slack&nbsp;
has a very different story.&nbsp;

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:12.000
While Mailchimp’s fall is mostly their own fault,&nbsp;
Slack was the best product, and still lost.&nbsp;

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:23.760
And it’s all thanks to Microsoft.
